{"name":"Heisenberg Blue","hex":"#70d4fb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Werner Heisenberg","blocks":[{"text":"Werner Karl Heisenberg was a German theoretical physicist, one of the main pioneers of the theory of quantum mechanics and a principal scientist in the German nuclear program during World War II."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Werner_Heisenberg_Portrait_%283x4_cropped%29.jpg/330px-Werner_Heisenberg_Portrait_%283x4_cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Werner_Heisenberg","wikidata":"Q40904"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c3/Heisenberg%2CWerner_1924_G%C3%B6ttingen_-_adjusted.jpeg/330px-Heisenberg%2CWerner_1924_G%C3%B6ttingen_-_adjusted.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c3/Heisenberg%2CWerner_1924_G%C3%B6ttingen_-_adjusted.jpeg/330px-Heisenberg%2CWerner_1924_G%C3%B6ttingen_-_adjusted.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Heisenberg in 1924","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHeisenberg%2CWerner_1924_G%C3%B6ttingen_-_adjusted.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Group_conversion_at_Copenhagen_Conference_in_1937_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Group_conversion_at_Copenhagen_Conference_in_1937_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Group_conversion_at_Copenhagen_Conference_in_1937_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Group_conversion_at_Copenhagen_Conference_in_1937_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Group_conversion_at_Copenhagen_Conference_in_1937_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Group_conversion_at_Copenhagen_Conference_in_1937_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Elisabeth Heisenberg (left) conversing with Niels Bohr and Werner Heisenberg, 1937","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGroup_conversion_at_Copenhagen_Conference_in_1937_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/UFission.gif/500px-UFission.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/UFission.gif/500px-UFission.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A visual representation of an induced nuclear fission event where a slow-moving neutron is absorbed by the nucleus of a uranium-235 atom, which fissions into two fast-moving lighter elements (fission products) and additional neutrons. Most of the energy released is in the form of the kinetic velocities of the fission products and the neutrons.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUFission.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Haigerloch-nuclear-reactor_ArM.JPG/500px-Haigerloch-nuclear-reactor_ArM.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Haigerloch-nuclear-reactor_ArM.JPG/1280px-Haigerloch-nuclear-reactor_ArM.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Haigerloch-nuclear-reactor_ArM.JPG/500px-Haigerloch-nuclear-reactor_ArM.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Replica of the German experimental nuclear reactor captured and dismantled at Haigerloch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHaigerloch-nuclear-reactor_ArM.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Werner_Heisenberg_bust.jpg/500px-Werner_Heisenberg_bust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Werner_Heisenberg_bust.jpg/1280px-Werner_Heisenberg_bust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Werner_Heisenberg_bust.jpg/500px-Werner_Heisenberg_bust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bust of Heisenberg in his old age, on display at the Max Planck Society campus in Garching bei München ","link":"htt